---
// ABOUTME: Double transposition tool page: encrypt/decrypt with two keywords, and a breaker given the key lengths.
// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Michel Oosterhof
// SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CipherTool from "../components/CipherTool.astro";
import AnnealBreaker from "../components/AnnealBreaker.astro";
import Faq from "../components/Faq.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Double transposition cipher — encrypt, decrypt, and break online"
  description="Encrypt, decrypt, and break the double transposition cipher online: columnar transposition applied twice with two keywords. Runs in your browser."
>
  <h1 class="mb-4 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">
    Double transposition cipher
  </h1>
  <p class="mb-8 max-w-prose text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
    Double transposition applies columnar transposition twice: the text is
    written in rows under the first keyword and read out by its columns in
    alphabetical order, then the result is transposed again under the second
    keyword. Every character, including spaces and punctuation, takes part; the
    last row may be incomplete (no padding).
  </p>
  <CipherTool cipher="double" />

  <h2 class="mt-12 mb-4 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">
    Break without the keys
  </h2>
  <p class="mb-6 max-w-prose text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
    Give the two key lengths and paste the ciphertext; the solver searches both
    column orders at once by simulated annealing, in parallel on your processor
    cores, and stops when the decryption reads as English. It works best on
    letters-only ciphertext of 200 letters or more; keys up to about seven
    columns are found quickly, longer keys need more text and several attempts.
  </p>
  <AnnealBreaker cipher="double" />

  <h2 class="mt-12 mb-4 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">
    About the double transposition cipher
  </h2>
  <div class="flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-4 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
    <p>
      A single columnar transposition falls quickly to anagramming, as the
      solver on the{" "}
      <a
        href="/transposition"
        class="text-orange-700 underline-offset-2 hover:underline dark:text-orange-400"
      >
        columnar transposition page
      </a>{" "}
      shows. Transposing twice destroys the column structure that attack relies
      on, because the second pass scatters every column of the first across the
      whole message. With two keys of moderate length, double transposition was
      strong enough to serve as a field cipher for armies and resistance
      networks through both world wars.
    </p>
    <p>
      It was broken in practice, but mainly through operational mistakes:
      several messages of the same length under the same keys can be anagrammed
      together, and a guessed plaintext fragment constrains both permutations.
      Modern statistical attacks, like the solver above, search both column
      orders at once; they need the key lengths, or a search over them, and a
      few hundred letters. Using the same keyword twice is allowed but makes the
      system weaker.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Faq
    items={[
      {
        question: "How does double transposition work?",
        answer:
          "It applies columnar transposition twice, first under one keyword and then under a second keyword. Decryption undoes the second transposition first, then the first.",
      },
      {
        question: "How secure is double transposition?",
        answer:
          "It resists hand solution far better than single transposition and served armies and agents through both world wars. It was broken mainly through key reuse: several messages of the same length under the same keys can be solved together.",
      },
      {
        question: "How do I break a double transposition without the keys?",
        answer:
          "Enter the two key lengths in the breaker on this page. It searches both column orders together by simulated annealing, scoring each attempt by how English the decryption reads. Keys up to about seven columns fall from 200 letters; longer keys need more text. If the lengths are unknown, try likely pairs in turn.",
      },
    ]}
  />
</Base>
